let's head to kabul airport now where  our correspondent charlotte bellas is  live for us charlotte so domestic  flights have been operating the past  week or so  what about those efforts to open the  airport for international flights  well they're still working to get  international flights up and running the  domestic flights are the big focus at  the moment the first uh flight from  ariana took off about a week ago but  this morning's significant because  kamir's flight just took off about 40  minutes ago to her they're the biggest  commercial carrier so real sign that  operations are starting to get back to  normal talking to people involved in the  airport operations this morning they say  about five flights are taking off each  day that i can tell you there are a lot  of people in the  departures terminal there's an arrivals  terminal they tell us that they've got  air traffic control functional up and  running if you can see behind me this is  the radar that was actually broken and  they've managed to fix it they say it's  not 100  but they're definitely getting there  making improvements every day so  domestic flights getting more momentum  you can see this plane over here this is  a humanitarian a plane that's in from  the gulf with humanitarian aid this  morning we just saw a military flight  also take off so there is  quite a lot of action uh on the tarmac  at the moment now now about three and a  half weeks since the taliban have taken  over taliban members are down here  patrolling they're involved in airport  operations but also working with foreign  allies the qataris and others  to look at security around the perimeter  in particular we've seen them patrolling  around the tarmac this morning so things  are slowly getting back to normal people  can fly domestically around the country  but certainly not at the levels they  were before  and i believe the taliban have  introduced new rules regarding people's  ability to protest  what exactly does that involve  protests have been  a key  part of the news this week here i mean  they were in smaller numbers talking a  dozen people 20 people 30 people but  then a couple of days ago there was a  large protest that was quite peaceful  for quite a few kilometers as they  marched through the city but then the  taliban as they came close to the  presidential palace they sent trucks out  started firing in the air people were  detained organizers of the protests were  detained journalists were detained their  cameras broken we know now since that  point there was another protest as well  and journalists were detained and  actually beaten under custody some  horrific photos coming out of some  journalists who were separated in uh  prison cells and then whipped with  cables their backs  lashed uh it's really uh brutal and hard  to um hard to look at type photos so  they are getting a lot of criticism for  that the taliban have since come out  banning protests but they say they will  allow some protests under a couple of  conditions those being you need to get  minister permission from the ministry of  justice you have to give them 24 hours  notice and you have to tell the ministry  the purpose time and place so it's going  to be interesting to see  if these if protests can continue and  what type of scope they give people  because for the moment they are really  cracking down on protesters and those  covering them  many thanks for your reporting charlotte  bellas there for us at kabul airport
 

